---
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Opsydyn — Open-source developer tools"
  description="Open-source tools for architecture, quality, and the terminal. TypeScript and Rust."
>
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <div class="site-container">
      <section class="hero">
        <h1 data-page-title>
          Precise tools<br />for<br />professionals. 
        </h1>
        <p class="tagline">
          Open-source tools for visualising architecture, enforcing quality,
          and building for the terminal. TypeScript and Rust.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a href="/projects" class="action-primary">
            <Icon name="blocks" size={16} /> View projects
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/opsydyn"
            class="action-secondary"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            <Icon name="git-branch" size={16} /> GitHub
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="page-section prose">
        <h2>Map what you build</h2>
        <p>
          Architecture decisions rot when they live only in people's heads.
          <code>c4-board</code> gives you a visual C4 modelling board built on Tauri
          and Astro. <code>tech-radar-web</code> tracks your technology choices as a
          living document. <code>tech-radar-tui</code> brings ADR authoring and radar
          management to the terminal — keyboard-driven, written in Rust.
        </p>
        <h2>Raise the bar</h2>
        <p>
          <code>elysia-spectral</code> enforces OpenAPI quality at the Elysia framework
          level, catching contract drift before it ships. <code>planetnik</code> makes
          large TypeScript codebases navigable through static analysis.
          <code>crumbs-css</code> brings React Native's styling ergonomics to the
          browser with a vanilla-extract-shaped API.
        </p>
        <h2>Open source, MIT</h2>
        <p>
          Everything is MIT-licensed and developed in the open by{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/alanpcurrie" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alan Currie</a>.
          If you're going to build a system around a tool, you should be able to read it.
          All projects live in public repositories — issues, discussions, and
          contributions are welcome.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
